[2026-03-16T04:02:28.862Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018TJpQBeqDpx7uB2tLmXYCM","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→During this stage you will polish the app to improve its quality, identifying and fixing\n     4→issues around the app's appearance, performance, code structure, and so on.\n     5→\n     6→## Progress Tracking\n     7→\n     8→Polish work is tracked in `docs/plan.md`. Each polish stage has a section in the file with\n     9→a checklist of completed work. Before unpacking subtasks, read `docs/plan.md` (create it\n    10→if it doesn't exist) and check which stages have already been completed.\n    11→\n    12→The format is:\n    13→\n    14→```markdown\n    15→## Polish\n    16→\n    17→### Responsive UI\n    18→- [x] ClientsListPage\n    19→- [x] ClientDetailPage\n    20→- [ ] DealsListPage\n    21→```\n    22→\n    23→When a polish stage is fully complete, mark it done:\n    24→\n    25→```markdown\n    26→### Responsive UI ✓\n    27→```\n    28→\n    29→A stage with `✓` in its heading is skipped during unpacking — no tasks are added for it.\n    30→\n    31→## Unpack Subtasks\n    32→\n    33→Read `docs/plan.md` and check which polish stages still need work. For each incomplete stage,\n    34→add the appropriate tasks as described below. After adding tasks, update `docs/plan.md` with\n    35→the planned items (unchecked). As each subtask completes, check off its entry.\n    36→\n    37→### Accessibility\n    38→\n    39→If the `Accessibility` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\n    40→accessibility work. Read `docs/tests.md` to identify all pages, then add ALL page tasks in a\n    41→single `add-task` call — write out every page explicitly, do not use a loop or script:\n    42→\n    43→```bash\n    44→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    45→[\n    46→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeAccessible<Page1>: Make <Page1> accessible\"] },\n    47→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeAccessible<Page2>: Make <Page2> accessible\"] }\n    48→]\n    49→EOF\n    50→```\n    51→\n    52→Add or update the `Accessibility` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for each page.\n    53→\n    54→### Responsive UI\n    55→\n    56→If the `Responsive UI` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\n    57→responsive work. Read `docs/tests.md` to identify all pages, then add ALL page tasks in a\n    58→single `add-task` call — write out every page explicitly, do not use a loop or script:\n    59→\n    60→```bash\n    61→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    62→[\n    63→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeResponsive<Page1>: Make <Page1> responsive\"] },\n    64→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeResponsive<Page2>: Make <Page2> responsive\"] }\n    65→]\n    66→EOF\n    67→```\n    68→\n    69→Add or update the `Responsive UI` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for each page.\n    70→\n    71→### Performance\n    72→\n    73→If the `Performance` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\n    74→performance work. Add a single task for the backend API review:\n    75→\n    76→```bash\n    77→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    78→[\n    79→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"PaginateAPIs: Review and paginate backend APIs that return large datasets\"] }\n    80→]\n    81→EOF\n    82→```\n    83→\n    84→Add or update the `Performance` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for the API review.\n    85→\n    86→## Making a Page Accessible\n    87→\n    88→When working on a `MakeAccessible` subtask, audit the page and its components for accessibility\n    89→issues and fix them. The goal is that all functionality is usable via keyboard alone and that\n    90→screen readers can navigate and understand all content.\n    91→\n    92→After completing the work, check off the page in `docs/plan.md`. If all pages are done, mark the\n    93→section heading with `✓`.\n    94→\n    95→### Semantic HTML\n    96→\n    97→- Use semantic elements (`<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<aside>`) instead\n    98→  of bare `<div>`s for page structure. Each page's content area should be wrapped in `<main>`.\n    99→- Use `<button>` for clickable actions and `<a>` for navigation. Never attach click handlers to\n   100→  `<div>` or `<span>` elements.\n   101→- Use heading elements (`<h1>`–`<h6>`) in proper hierarchical order — one `<h1>` per page, no\n   102→  skipped levels.\n   103→- Use `<ul>`/`<ol>` for lists of items, `<table>` for tabular data.\n   104→\n   105→### Keyboard navigation\n   106→\n   107→- All interactive elements must be reachable via Tab and activatable via Enter/Space.\n   108→- Modals and dialogs must trap focus while open and return focus to the trigger element on close.\n   109→- Dropdown menus and popovers must support Escape to close and arrow keys to navigate options.\n   110→- Visible focus indicators must be present on all focusable elements — never set `outline: none`\n   111→  without providing an alternative focus style.\n   112→\n   113→### ARIA attributes\n   114→\n   115→- Add `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` to interactive elements that lack visible text labels\n   116→  (icon-only buttons, icon links, search inputs).\n   117→- Use `role=\"dialog\"` and `aria-modal=\"true\"` on modal containers with `aria-labelledby` pointing\n   118→  to the modal title.\n   119→- Use `aria-expanded` on buttons that toggle collapsible sections or dropdowns.\n   120→- Use `aria-current=\"page\"` on the active navigation link in sidebars/navbars.\n   121→- Add `aria-live=\"polite\"` to regions that update dynamically (toast notifications, status messages,\n   122→  loading states).\n   123→- Use `aria-describedby` to associate error messages with their form fields.\n   124→\n   125→### Forms\n   126→\n   127→- Every form input must have an associated `<label>` element (via `htmlFor`) or an `aria-label`.\n   128→- Group related fields with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>` where appropriate.\n   129→- Display validation errors adjacent to the relevant field with `role=\"alert\"` or linked via\n   130→  `aria-describedby`.\n   131→- Required fields should use the `aria-required=\"true\"` attribute.\n   132→\n   133→### Color and contrast\n   134→\n   135→- Text must meet WCAG AA contrast ratios: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text (18px+ bold\n   136→  or 24px+ regular).\n   137→- Never convey information through color alone — pair colors with icons, text labels, or patterns\n   138→  (e.g., status badges should include text, not just a colored dot).\n   139→- Focus indicators must have at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colors.\n   140→\n   141→### Images and media\n   142→\n   143→- All `<img>` elements must have an `alt` attribute. Decorative images use `alt=\"\"`.\n   144→- Icons used as the sole content of a button or link need `aria-label` on the parent element or\n   145→  `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on the icon with a visually hidden text label alongside it.\n   146→\n   147→## Making a Page Responsive\n   148→\n   149→When working on a `MakeResponsive` subtask, update the page and its components so the layout adapts\n   150→cleanly as the viewport shrinks from desktop down to mobile widths. The goal is that all content\n   151→remains usable and readable — no horizontal scrolling, no overlapping elements, no truncated\n   152→controls.\n   153→\n   154→After completing the work, check off the page in `docs/plan.md`. If all pages are done, mark the\n   155→section heading with `✓`.\n   156→\n   157→### Implementation approach\n   158→\n   159→Use **Tailwind responsive utility classes** (max-width variants) as the primary tool. Only fall\n   160→back to raw CSS media queries in `index.css` when Tailwind utilities cannot express the rule\n   161→(e.g., hiding table columns rendered from mapped data, or switching `<tr>` display modes).\n   162→\n   163→**Do not mix min-width and max-width variants.** Use `max-*:` variants consistently so styles\n   164→degrade from desktop down:\n   165→- `max-lg:` — max-width 1024px\n   166→- `max-md:` — max-width 768px\n   167→- `max-sm:` — max-width 640px\n   168→\n   169→When raw CSS is needed (e.g., table column hiding via nth-child or tag selectors), use `@media\n   170→(max-width: ...)` at 1024px, 768px, and 480px. The 480px CSS breakpoint covers the gap below\n   171→Tailwind's 640px `max-sm:`.\n   172→\n   173→Do not use JavaScript-based layout switching. Do not add a hamburger menu or mobile navigation\n   174→unless the app already has one — the sidebar stays fixed.\n   175→\n   176→### Layout adjustments\n   177→\n   178→Apply these patterns at the Tailwind breakpoints:\n   179→\n   180→- **Page padding**: `p-6 max-sm:p-3` on the outermost page wrapper.\n   181→- **Multi-column grids** (detail page bodies, metadata grids): start with the desktop column\n   182→  count and collapse at each breakpoint.\n   183→  - Two-column body: `grid grid-cols-2 max-md:grid-cols-1 gap-4`\n   184→  - Four-column metadata: `grid grid-cols-4 max-md:grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1 gap-4`\n   185→- **Section card padding**: `p-4 max-sm:p-3` or `px-5 max-sm:px-3`.\n   186→- **Form layouts** with side-by-side fields: `grid grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1 gap-4`.\n   187→- **Typography scaling**: reduce heading sizes at `max-sm:`.\n   188→  - Page titles: `text-[24px] max-sm:text-[20px]`\n   189→  - Detail headings: `text-[18px] max-sm:text-[16px]`\n   190→- **Button text**: on narrow viewports, hide button labels and keep only icons:\n   191→  `<span className=\"max-sm:hidden\">Label</span>`\n   192→- **Horizontal button rows** and action bars: use `flex-wrap` so they wrap naturally.\n   193→- **Modals and dialogs**: constrain with `max-sm:max-w-[calc(100%-24px)]` and ensure\n   194→  `overflow-y-auto` for tall content.\n   195→\n   196→### Data density in lists and tables\n   197→\n   198→Lists and tables are the most important thing to get right. On wide viewports they can show many\n   199→columns, but on narrow viewports they must progressively hide less-important columns.\n   200→\n   201→- Identify which columns are essential (e.g., name, status) vs secondary (e.g., created date,\n   202→  assigned user, tags).\n   203→- **Column hiding**: Use Tailwind `max-lg:hidden` and `max-md:hidden` directly on `<th>`/`<td>`\n   204→  elements when the table is rendered with explicit column elements. When columns are generated\n   205→  from mapped data or use CSS Grid, define named classes in `index.css` with `@media` rules.\n   206→- **Card mode at narrowest viewport**: At 480px (via `@media` in `index.css`), hide the table\n   207→  header and switch rows to `display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap` so each row reads as a card with\n   208→  key fields only.\n   209→- Ensure sort/filter controls remain accessible — use `flex-wrap` on filter bars so controls\n   210→  wrap naturally on narrow viewports.\n   211→\n   212→### Card-based lists\n   213→\n   214→When the page uses cards instead of a table (e.g., task lists), adjust information density within\n   215→each card at breakpoints:\n   216→\n   217→- At `max-lg:` hide secondary metadata (avatars, role text).\n   218→- At `max-md:` relocate metadata that was in a side column to an inline row below the title\n   219→  using `hidden max-md:flex`.\n   220→- At `max-sm:` consolidate remaining metadata into a single compact row using\n   221→  `hidden max-sm:flex`.\n   222→\n   223→## Paginating Backend APIs\n   224→\n   225→When working on a `PaginateAPIs` subtask, review every backend API function (Netlify function or\n   226→equivalent) that returns a list of records. Any endpoint that could return an unbounded or large\n   227→number of rows must be updated to limit the data returned per request.\n   228→\n   229→After completing the work, check off the entry in `docs/plan.md`. If all performance items are\n   230→done, mark the section heading with `✓`.\n   231→\n   232→### Identifying endpoints to fix\n   233→\n   234→Read all backend function files and find every endpoint that queries a list of records (e.g.,\n   235→`SELECT * FROM ...` without a `LIMIT`, or an ORM call like `.findMany()` / `.select()` that\n   236→returns all matching rows). Endpoints that return a single record by ID or a small fixed set\n   237→(e.g., enum values, current user) can be skipped.\n   238→\n   239→### Choosing a strategy\n   240→\n   241→Pick the approach that fits how the frontend consumes the data:\n   242→\n   243→- **Cursor-based pagination** (preferred for lists the user scrolls through): return a `cursor`\n   244→  (typically the last row's ID or sort key) and a `hasMore` flag. The frontend requests the next\n   245→  page by passing the cursor back. This avoids the count query and handles concurrent inserts\n   246→  gracefully.\n   247→- **Offset/limit pagination** (acceptable when the UI shows page numbers): return `items`,\n   248→  `total`, `page`, and `pageSize`. Use `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` in the query.\n   249→- **Chunked loading** (for data that must all be present client-side, e.g., chart data, reports):\n   250→  if the total dataset can grow large but the frontend needs it all, break it into smaller\n   251→  sequential requests by date range or category rather than loading everything in one call.\n   252→\n   253→### Implementation guidelines\n   254→\n   255→- Default page size should be reasonable for the data type — typically 20–50 rows for table/list\n   256→  views, up to 100 for lightweight records.\n   257→- Accept `pageSize` / `limit` as a query parameter but enforce a maximum (e.g., 100) to prevent\n   258→  abuse.\n   259→- Always apply an `ORDER BY` to ensure stable pagination ordering.\n   260→- Update the corresponding frontend code to pass pagination parameters and handle paginated\n   261→  responses (load-more buttons, infinite scroll, or page controls as appropriate for the UI).\n   262→- Update any frontend code that assumes all records are returned at once (e.g., client-side\n   263→  filtering/sorting across the full dataset) — either move that logic server-side or fetch all\n   264→  pages when needed for search/filter.\n   265→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89faff73-b689-4266-95e8-94a07c37d050","uuid":"93f9137a-9151-46e9-8ff9-3190a4d81820","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md","content":"# Skill\n\nDuring this stage you will polish the app to improve its quality, identifying and fixing\nissues around the app's appearance, performance, code structure, and so on.\n\n## Progress Tracking\n\nPolish work is tracked in `docs/plan.md`. Each polish stage has a section in the file with\na checklist of completed work. Before unpacking subtasks, read `docs/plan.md` (create it\nif it doesn't exist) and check which stages have already been completed.\n\nThe format is:\n\n```markdown\n## Polish\n\n### Responsive UI\n- [x] ClientsListPage\n- [x] ClientDetailPage\n- [ ] DealsListPage\n```\n\nWhen a polish stage is fully complete, mark it done:\n\n```markdown\n### Responsive UI ✓\n```\n\nA stage with `✓` in its heading is skipped during unpacking — no tasks are added for it.\n\n## Unpack Subtasks\n\nRead `docs/plan.md` and check which polish stages still need work. For each incomplete stage,\nadd the appropriate tasks as described below. After adding tasks, update `docs/plan.md` with\nthe planned items (unchecked). As each subtask completes, check off its entry.\n\n### Accessibility\n\nIf the `Accessibility` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\naccessibility work. Read `docs/tests.md` to identify all pages, then add ALL page tasks in a\nsingle `add-task` call — write out every page explicitly, do not use a loop or script:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeAccessible<Page1>: Make <Page1> accessible\"] },\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeAccessible<Page2>: Make <Page2> accessible\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nAdd or update the `Accessibility` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for each page.\n\n### Responsive UI\n\nIf the `Responsive UI` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\nresponsive work. Read `docs/tests.md` to identify all pages, then add ALL page tasks in a\nsingle `add-task` call — write out every page explicitly, do not use a loop or script:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeResponsive<Page1>: Make <Page1> responsive\"] },\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeResponsive<Page2>: Make <Page2> responsive\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nAdd or update the `Responsive UI` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for each page.\n\n### Performance\n\nIf the `Performance` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\nperformance work. Add a single task for the backend API review:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"PaginateAPIs: Review and paginate backend APIs that return large datasets\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nAdd or update the `Performance` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for the API review.\n\n## Making a Page Accessible\n\nWhen working on a `MakeAccessible` subtask, audit the page and its components for accessibility\nissues and fix them. The goal is that all functionality is usable via keyboard alone and that\nscreen readers can navigate and understand all content.\n\nAfter completing the work, check off the page in `docs/plan.md`. If all pages are done, mark the\nsection heading with `✓`.\n\n### Semantic HTML\n\n- Use semantic elements (`<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<aside>`) instead\n  of bare `<div>`s for page structure. Each page's content area should be wrapped in `<main>`.\n- Use `<button>` for clickable actions and `<a>` for navigation. Never attach click handlers to\n  `<div>` or `<span>` elements.\n- Use heading elements (`<h1>`–`<h6>`) in proper hierarchical order — one `<h1>` per page, no\n  skipped levels.\n- Use `<ul>`/`<ol>` for lists of items, `<table>` for tabular data.\n\n### Keyboard navigation\n\n- All interactive elements must be reachable via Tab and activatable via Enter/Space.\n- Modals and dialogs must trap focus while open and return focus to the trigger element on close.\n- Dropdown menus and popovers must support Escape to close and arrow keys to navigate options.\n- Visible focus indicators must be present on all focusable elements — never set `outline: none`\n  without providing an alternative focus style.\n\n### ARIA attributes\n\n- Add `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` to interactive elements that lack visible text labels\n  (icon-only buttons, icon links, search inputs).\n- Use `role=\"dialog\"` and `aria-modal=\"true\"` on modal containers with `aria-labelledby` pointing\n  to the modal title.\n- Use `aria-expanded` on buttons that toggle collapsible sections or dropdowns.\n- Use `aria-current=\"page\"` on the active navigation link in sidebars/navbars.\n- Add `aria-live=\"polite\"` to regions that update dynamically (toast notifications, status messages,\n  loading states).\n- Use `aria-describedby` to associate error messages with their form fields.\n\n### Forms\n\n- Every form input must have an associated `<label>` element (via `htmlFor`) or an `aria-label`.\n- Group related fields with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>` where appropriate.\n- Display validation errors adjacent to the relevant field with `role=\"alert\"` or linked via\n  `aria-describedby`.\n- Required fields should use the `aria-required=\"true\"` attribute.\n\n### Color and contrast\n\n- Text must meet WCAG AA contrast ratios: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text (18px+ bold\n  or 24px+ regular).\n- Never convey information through color alone — pair colors with icons, text labels, or patterns\n  (e.g., status badges should include text, not just a colored dot).\n- Focus indicators must have at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colors.\n\n### Images and media\n\n- All `<img>` elements must have an `alt` attribute. Decorative images use `alt=\"\"`.\n- Icons used as the sole content of a button or link need `aria-label` on the parent element or\n  `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on the icon with a visually hidden text label alongside it.\n\n## Making a Page Responsive\n\nWhen working on a `MakeResponsive` subtask, update the page and its components so the layout adapts\ncleanly as the viewport shrinks from desktop down to mobile widths. The goal is that all content\nremains usable and readable — no horizontal scrolling, no overlapping elements, no truncated\ncontrols.\n\nAfter completing the work, check off the page in `docs/plan.md`. If all pages are done, mark the\nsection heading with `✓`.\n\n### Implementation approach\n\nUse **Tailwind responsive utility classes** (max-width variants) as the primary tool. Only fall\nback to raw CSS media queries in `index.css` when Tailwind utilities cannot express the rule\n(e.g., hiding table columns rendered from mapped data, or switching `<tr>` display modes).\n\n**Do not mix min-width and max-width variants.** Use `max-*:` variants consistently so styles\ndegrade from desktop down:\n- `max-lg:` — max-width 1024px\n- `max-md:` — max-width 768px\n- `max-sm:` — max-width 640px\n\nWhen raw CSS is needed (e.g., table column hiding via nth-child or tag selectors), use `@media\n(max-width: ...)` at 1024px, 768px, and 480px. The 480px CSS breakpoint covers the gap below\nTailwind's 640px `max-sm:`.\n\nDo not use JavaScript-based layout switching. Do not add a hamburger menu or mobile navigation\nunless the app already has one — the sidebar stays fixed.\n\n### Layout adjustments\n\nApply these patterns at the Tailwind breakpoints:\n\n- **Page padding**: `p-6 max-sm:p-3` on the outermost page wrapper.\n- **Multi-column grids** (detail page bodies, metadata grids): start with the desktop column\n  count and collapse at each breakpoint.\n  - Two-column body: `grid grid-cols-2 max-md:grid-cols-1 gap-4`\n  - Four-column metadata: `grid grid-cols-4 max-md:grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1 gap-4`\n- **Section card padding**: `p-4 max-sm:p-3` or `px-5 max-sm:px-3`.\n- **Form layouts** with side-by-side fields: `grid grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1 gap-4`.\n- **Typography scaling**: reduce heading sizes at `max-sm:`.\n  - Page titles: `text-[24px] max-sm:text-[20px]`\n  - Detail headings: `text-[18px] max-sm:text-[16px]`\n- **Button text**: on narrow viewports, hide button labels and keep only icons:\n  `<span className=\"max-sm:hidden\">Label</span>`\n- **Horizontal button rows** and action bars: use `flex-wrap` so they wrap naturally.\n- **Modals and dialogs**: constrain with `max-sm:max-w-[calc(100%-24px)]` and ensure\n  `overflow-y-auto` for tall content.\n\n### Data density in lists and tables\n\nLists and tables are the most important thing to get right. On wide viewports they can show many\ncolumns, but on narrow viewports they must progressively hide less-important columns.\n\n- Identify which columns are essential (e.g., name, status) vs secondary (e.g., created date,\n  assigned user, tags).\n- **Column hiding**: Use Tailwind `max-lg:hidden` and `max-md:hidden` directly on `<th>`/`<td>`\n  elements when the table is rendered with explicit column elements. When columns are generated\n  from mapped data or use CSS Grid, define named classes in `index.css` with `@media` rules.\n- **Card mode at narrowest viewport**: At 480px (via `@media` in `index.css`), hide the table\n  header and switch rows to `display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap` so each row reads as a card with\n  key fields only.\n- Ensure sort/filter controls remain accessible — use `flex-wrap` on filter bars so controls\n  wrap naturally on narrow viewports.\n\n### Card-based lists\n\nWhen the page uses cards instead of a table (e.g., task lists), adjust information density within\neach card at breakpoints:\n\n- At `max-lg:` hide secondary metadata (avatars, role text).\n- At `max-md:` relocate metadata that was in a side column to an inline row below the title\n  using `hidden max-md:flex`.\n- At `max-sm:` consolidate remaining metadata into a single compact row using\n  `hidden max-sm:flex`.\n\n## Paginating Backend APIs\n\nWhen working on a `PaginateAPIs` subtask, review every backend API function (Netlify function or\nequivalent) that returns a list of records. Any endpoint that could return an unbounded or large\nnumber of rows must be updated to limit the data returned per request.\n\nAfter completing the work, check off the entry in `docs/plan.md`. If all performance items are\ndone, mark the section heading with `✓`.\n\n### Identifying endpoints to fix\n\nRead all backend function files and find every endpoint that queries a list of records (e.g.,\n`SELECT * FROM ...` without a `LIMIT`, or an ORM call like `.findMany()` / `.select()` that\nreturns all matching rows). Endpoints that return a single record by ID or a small fixed set\n(e.g., enum values, current user) can be skipped.\n\n### Choosing a strategy\n\nPick the approach that fits how the frontend consumes the data:\n\n- **Cursor-based pagination** (preferred for lists the user scrolls through): return a `cursor`\n  (typically the last row's ID or sort key) and a `hasMore` flag. The frontend requests the next\n  page by passing the cursor back. This avoids the count query and handles concurrent inserts\n  gracefully.\n- **Offset/limit pagination** (acceptable when the UI shows page numbers): return `items`,\n  `total`, `page`, and `pageSize`. Use `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` in the query.\n- **Chunked loading** (for data that must all be present client-side, e.g., chart data, reports):\n  if the total dataset can grow large but the frontend needs it all, break it into smaller\n  sequential requests by date range or category rather than loading everything in one call.\n\n### Implementation guidelines\n\n- Default page size should be reasonable for the data type — typically 20–50 rows for table/list\n  views, up to 100 for lightweight records.\n- Accept `pageSize` / `limit` as a query parameter but enforce a maximum (e.g., 100) to prevent\n  abuse.\n- Always apply an `ORDER BY` to ensure stable pagination ordering.\n- Update the corresponding frontend code to pass pagination parameters and handle paginated\n  responses (load-more buttons, infinite scroll, or page controls as appropriate for the UI).\n- Update any frontend code that assumes all records are returned at once (e.g., client-side\n  filtering/sorting across the full dataset) — either move that logic server-side or fetch all\n  pages when needed for search/filter.\n","numLines":265,"startLine":1,"totalLines":265}}}
